Skill create_ai_task {
  label: 'Create a scheduled AI task'
  description: "Create or change a scheduled AI task: an analysis that runs on a schedule. Use when the user wants something to run regularly, even without saying 'AI task', or wants to change a task's source, schedule or acceptance criteria. Typical phrasings: 'run revenue by category every Monday', 'automate our weekly category review', 'check every morning that the data landed', 'move my task to 9 AM', 'Change the acceptance criteria', 'Preview the first result'. When the request also emails, posts to Slack or alerts, load add_ai_task_delivery in the same step. Do not use for a one-off answer or a send right now. Load this skill first, before writing any text or calling another tool, and settle everything from this conversation without recalling earlier chats."
  invocation: 'auto'
  disabled: H.git.is_production
  content: @md
    Don't call `fetch_conversation`, `fetch_conversation_messages`, `fetch_conversation_tool_call` or `search_organization_conversations` unless the request points to an earlier chat: earlier chats are other tasks, and copying their answers skips the user's choices.

    # Create a scheduled AI task

    Author an AI task and its schedule as a draft. Don't run it or send anything: it runs unattended after publishing, so settle the essentials now.

    The requester is ${H.current_user.h_email}: "me" and `run_as` mean this address.

    ## Flow

    Ask every question on an `ask_user` card, because a reply without a tool call ends the turn. Write only the cards and the report. Keep the user waiting as little as possible: put independent calls in one step.

    1. **First step.** In one step: load `write_ai_task`, read `schedules.aml`, `read_folder` the root and `settings/ai/tasks`, `lookup_aml_object` the label in snake_case, and ask the Schedule card if one is needed, so the user answers it while you search.
    2. **Schedule.** A run needs one day and one clock time. Ask when either is missing or a range ("Monday morning", "weekly", "end of day"); each option shows the time and the next run. Never ask the timezone.
    3. **Source.** Find every dataset with the fields: the `*.dataset.aml` files in every folder, opening all subfolders of a level in one step. When two or more have the fields, `fetch_dataset` them, then in one step run `execute_aql` on each with this query instead of `generate_aql`: `explore { measures { latest: <model> | max(<model>.<date field>) } }`. Ask a Source card listing each of them, stale ones included: "<label> · data through <Oct 4, 2026>", freshest first and recommended. A dataset with no rows in the latest complete period shows "data ends <date>" and is never saved, because a task on it never fires: if it is the only one, look further or ask. Skip the card when the user named the dataset. Also ask when two metrics fit, or the grouping has levels the words don't settle.
    4. **Delivery.** If the request emails, posts or alerts, `add_ai_task_delivery` must be loaded too. Otherwise ask nothing about sending: the report offers it as follow-ups.
    5. **Write.** Save, validate and report as `write_ai_task` shows.

    A card covers one part: the recommended option first, marked "(recommended)", then 1–3 real alternatives, with any rule to confirm (week boundaries, top N, ties) in the option's description. Fill other gaps with a sensible default stated in the brief. In a request that sends, also ask about vague words that change the result ("changed the most").

    > User: "Set up a weekly revenue by category analysis every Monday morning."
    > Cards: "Monday 8:00 AM (recommended)" in the first step, then Source (if two datasets fit) → report with follow-ups, no question about sending.

    > User: "Every Monday at 8am, email me which categories changed the most. Add a chart too."
    > Loads `add_ai_task_delivery`; one card, "Top 5 by dollar change (recommended)"; saves Email · requester, Viz · PNG; the report ends at Files.

    ## Change a task

    In one step, load `write_ai_task` and read the task file and `schedules.aml`. Settle only what changes and save only the changed files. For "Change the acceptance criteria", ask a card showing the current criteria, with 2–3 likely edits as options. For "Preview the first result", run the brief's Steps now and show the output and the table, sending and saving nothing.
  ;;
}
